          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Lorck's present loving state. His immediate insistence that it is serious and deeper than he himself had realized reinforces its dominant truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal forms the opening of an integrated offer of personal and relational self-dedication. The immediately following gift of himself, his life, and his soul, together with the request that Constance accept it, contextualizes “I love you” as more than a report: it affirms Lorck as bound to the offered bond. The terms remain figurative and unspecified, so undertaking force is substantial rather than fully determinate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nej, uf nej,\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is highly agitated, the focal words are a deliberate, propositionally explicit avowal renewed as part of an appeal for acceptance, not a reflex-like cry or affective discharge beyond assertion and undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"vil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence's principal functions are adequately represented by avowal and an integrated relational offer. Its appeal for acceptance belongs to the undertaking dimension rather than requiring a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Lorck has confessed a consuming passion and Constance has recoiled and challenged his conduct, he repeats the love declaration solemnly, expands it into an offer of himself, and asks for acceptance. Constance immediately refuses and retreats.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Lorck offers Constance himself, his life, and his soul, figuratively affirming comprehensive personal dedication and inviting her to accept a loving bond. The undertaking is integrated with this occurrence of the love declaration, though its practical obligations are unspecified. Constance explicitly rejects it with “Nej, uf nej,” and subsequently tells him he should leave, so there is no uptake.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied evidence identifies the work as Amalie Skram's Constance Ring, which I recognize; no omitted plot facts have been used.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Lorck as passionately affected, but it cannot conclusively establish his sincerity beyond the represented behavior and assertions. It also does not specify concrete terms, duration, or obligations for his figurative offer of self.\"}"
        }
